zlib: do not redefine fdopen on Apple platforms

gcc bundles a copy of zlib whose zutil.h treats TARGET_OS_MAC as classic
Mac OS and defines fdopen to NULL. On modern macOS __APPLE__ is defined
and fdopen is a real libc function, so that define collides with the SDK
declaration in <_stdio.h>:

  _stdio.h:322:7: error: expected identifier or '('

Only take the classic-Mac path when __APPLE__ is not defined.

Anchored with surrounding context rather than a bare line number. The
same hunk sits at line 133 in gcc's bundled zlib but line 140 in
binutils' copy, so a line-number-only hunk cannot be shared between them
and silently misapplies when versions move.

Signed-off-by: Ralic Lo <raliclo@gmail.com>

--- a/zlib/zutil.h
+++ b/zlib/zutil.h
@@ -130,7 +130,7 @@
 #  endif
 #endif

-#if defined(MACOS) || defined(TARGET_OS_MAC)
+#if (defined(MACOS) || defined(TARGET_OS_MAC)) && !defined(__APPLE__)
 #  define OS_CODE  7
 #  ifndef Z_SOLO
 #    if defined(__MWERKS__) && __dest_os != __be_os && __dest_os != __win32_os
